FOURTH ROUND (2026-11-21 05:25): what the firmware side does with channel 3
================================================================================

Pulled the vendor wcn_bsp.ko as well (/vendor_dlkm/lib/modules/wcn_bsp.ko, 1365238 B,
sha256 5cd978b5...). Three things it settles:

8. mtty_write's bus operations are the same calls ours makes.

      2374: ldr x20, [x0, #48]   ; list_alloc
      23c8: ldr x8,  [x0, #64]   ; push_list   (not push_list_direct, which is 72)
      25e4: ldr x22, [x0, #56]   ; list_free, error path only

   sprdwcn_bus_ops order: preinit 0, deinit 8, chn_init 16, chn_deinit 24, get_hwintf_type 32,
   get_bus_status 40, list_alloc 48, list_free 56, push_list 64, push_list_direct 72.

9. marlin_set_power(MARLIN_BLUETOOTH, 1) does nothing to the chip when Wi-Fi is already up.

   Branch 3 of wcn_boot.c fires because power_state & AUTO_RUN_MASK is non-zero, and it only
   calls set_wifipa_status(), set_bit(MARLIN_BLUETOOTH, power_state) and the notify. No
   download, no register write, no power domain. So BT is expected to be a part of the btwifi
   firmware that Wi-Fi's download already brought up, and start_marlin() for BT is bookkeeping.
   That is why "bluetooth_set_power: end_block: 0, ret: 0" looks like success while nothing
   happens on the card.

   Related: the BTRAM power helper returns early for this chip, which is correct - the DT
   compatible is "unisoc,marlin3lite_sdio" and that maps to .unisoc_wcn_m3lite.

   Firmware on both sides is identical: wcnmodem.bin 3145728, gnssmodem.bin 595970,
   bt_configure_pskey.ini 5442, bt_configure_rf.ini 3115. Android also carries _aa / .xpe
   variants of the two .ini files; those change PSKey contents, not whether the card answers.

10. The one remaining lead is on the firmware side.

   In the boot where bluetooth-start still did rfkill block first, the firmware asserted with

       WCN BASE: fw assert:WCN Assert in pseudo_atc_main.c line 971, rd_len <= PSEUDO_ATC_BUFF_SIZE

   pseudo_atc_main.c is firmware. The 184 bytes we wrote on channel 3 were taken by the
   pseudo-ATC parser rather than the HCI one, which is consistent with no HCI event ever coming
   back. Next: either channel 3 is not BT TX after all (re-interpret the vendor .data), or the
   frame needs the pseudo-ATC header. Sending one AT command through /proc/mdbg/at_cmd and
   getting a reply would prove the host-to-firmware control path independently, leaving the
   channel number as the only variable.

   Android's dmesg cannot help here: with BT working normally, "dmesg | grep -ic 'mtty.*rx'"
   is 0, because the vendor log level hides the RX path.
