SEVENTH ROUND (2026-11-21 06:10): the channel number cannot be the problem
================================================================================

18. A channel sweep closes the whole "wrong channel" hypothesis space.

    A module parameter mu300_diag (off by default) makes mtty_open put its TX channel in a
    variable and then, once powered, walk every channel the BT block could plausibly use:

        RX: chn_init on 16..25          -> 16, 18, 19, 20, 21, 25 accepted
                                           (17 ours, 22/23/24 Wi-Fi)
        TX: chn_init on 1..11, then one HCI Reset per accepted channel
                                        -> 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9, 11 accepted
                                           (3 ours, 8/10 Wi-Fi)
        8 x "hci_reset_write=4", 6 channels listening, "MU300-BT rx_cb" count 0.

    Eight different bus subtypes sent HCI Reset with receivers registered on six channels and
    not one reply came back. The controller's BT function is not consuming HCI data at all;
    which number the channel carries is irrelevant.

19. Firmware and clock type are also ruled out.

        wcnmodem.bin   844dc10209cf39e9ff5ff0638839c8922d74798fb58c3a9ff2151bf532c87060
        gnssmodem.bin  85e1a6d1ec79e40029f44743663bd9277e462cfc9313fd9fbf1d9c09160491fd

    identical to Android's /odm/firmware copies. And although our /proc/cmdline has no
    "marlin.clktype=" (Android carries marlin.clktype=0), wcn_boot.c only uses it to pick TCXO
    vs TSX for the 26 MHz crystal, and Wi-Fi runs on that same crystal - so the effective clock
    type is right.

    What is left is a BT enable that the Android HAL performs and we have not reproduced: the
    vendor module's chipid / dumpmem / bluetooth_reset sysfs group plus its wcn_reset_notifier
    registration, none of which exist in the realme source we build from.